Classic old-fashioned bakery-style ginger cookies. Creaming the butter and sugar for a full 5–7 minutes creates a puffy, tender cookie that stays soft for days.

*Adapted from [Taste of Home](https://www.tasteofhome.com/recipes/big-soft-ginger-cookies/print/).*

## Ingredients

- ¾ cup unsalted butter, softened to room temperature
- 1 cup granulated sugar (plus ¼ cup extra for rolling)
- 1 large egg, room temperature
- ¼ cup dark molasses
- 2 ¼ cups all-purpose flour
- 2 teaspoons ground ginger
- 1 teaspoon baking soda
- ¾ teaspoon ground cinnamon
- ½ teaspoon ground cloves
- ¼ teaspoon salt

## Instructions

1. **Cream the Butter and Sugar:** In a large bowl (or stand mixer fitted with the paddle attachment), beat the softened butter and 1 cup granulated sugar on medium speed for 5 to 7 minutes until pale, light, and fluffy.
2. **Add Egg and Molasses:** Beat in the egg and dark molasses until thoroughly incorporated, scraping down the sides of the bowl.
3. **Mix the Dry Ingredients:** In a separate medium bowl, whisk together the flour, ground ginger, baking soda, cinnamon, cloves, and salt.
4. **Form the Dough:** Gradually add the flour mixture to the creamed butter, mixing on low speed just until no streaks remain.
5. **Shape and Coat:** Scoop the dough and roll into 1 ½-inch balls. Place additional granulated sugar in a shallow dish and roll each dough ball until generously coated in sparkling sugar.
6. **Bake:** Arrange the balls 2 inches apart on ungreased baking sheets (or parchment-lined sheets). Bake at 350°F (175°C) for 10 to 12 minutes. The cookies will be puffy with crinkled tops and soft centers. Do not overbake.
7. **Cool:** Allow cookies to sit on the hot baking sheets for 2 minutes before transferring to wire racks to cool completely.

## Notes

- **Secret to Softness:** The generous creaming time (5 to 7 minutes) aerates the butter base and prevents the cookies from baking up dense or flat.
- **Storage:** Keep in an airtight container with a slice of sandwich bread; they will stay soft and chewy for over a week.
